<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ali Qapu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ali Qapu is a palace on the western side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-jahan-square">Naqsh-e Jahan Square</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, built under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> around the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Abbas I</a>. It served as a grand gateway and reception palace, with an elevated veranda from which the shah viewed events in the square. It is a celebrated example of Safavid architecture and decoration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ali-qapu.jpg" alt="Ali Qapu" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amir Pashaei · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C4%80l%C4%AB_Q%C4%81p%C5%AB_in_golden_time.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ali Qapu, a name meaning Grand Gate, was originally designed as a vast portal entrance to the grand palace that stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-jahan-square">Naqsh-e Jahan Square</a> to the Chaharbagh Boulevard. The name combines the Arabic ʿālī, meaning superior or imperial, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-turkic">Old Turkic</a> qāpū, meaning door. It stands on the western side of the square, opposite the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-lotfollah-mosque">Sheikh Lotfollah Mosque</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> has inscribed the palace and the square as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. The building was raised by decree of Shah Abbas the Great in the early 17th century, and the Safavid monarchs used it to entertain noble visitors and foreign ambassadors.</p><p>It was built in stages over about seventy years. The first was a cubical two-storey entrance building measuring 20 by 19 metres and 13 metres high. Upper halls were then added above the vestibule, followed by a music hall, an eastern veranda advancing towards the square, and a wooden balcony ceiling on 18 columns. A stairway on the southern flank, called the Kingly Stairway, and a water tower for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> pool of the columned balcony followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The palace is forty-eight metres high and has six floors, each reached by a difficult spiral staircase. The chancellery stood on the first floor. Royal receptions and banquets were held on the sixth floor, which holds the largest rooms and was popularly called the Music Hall, where ensembles played and sang. Deep circular niches in its walls have an acoustic as well as a decorative value, and its stucco decoration is full of vessels and cups. From the upper galleries the shahs watched polo, army manoeuvres and horse races in the square.</p><p>Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-ii-of-persia">Abbas II</a> expanded and renovated the palace, and his main addition was the grand hall on the third floor, with 18 mirror-covered columns and a painted ceiling. The interior is decorated with naturalistic wall paintings of flowers, animals and birds by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-abbasi">Reza Abbasi</a>, the court painter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a>, and his pupils.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Shah Soltan Hoseyn, the last Safavid ruler, had the palace substantially restored, but it fell into disrepair again during the short reign of the invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>. Most of its richly ornamented doors and windows were looted in times of disorder, and only one window on the third floor survives. Under Naser ad-Din Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> the Safavid cornices and floral tiles above the portal were replaced by tiles bearing inscriptions. The palace is regarded as the best example of Safavid architecture and appears on the reverse of the Iranian 20,000 rial banknote.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ali Qapu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Isfahan's ensemble of Naqsh-e Jahan Square, the Shah Mosque, the Sheikh Lotfollah Mosque and the <b>Ali Qapu</b> palace is one of the supreme achievements of Islamic urban design.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Abbas built an army paid by and loyal to himself, brought provinces under his direct rule and in 1598 moved the capital to Isfahan, where Naqsh-e Jahan Square set the mosque, the bazaar and his own <b>Ali Qapu</b> palace side by side.</blockquote><a href="/societies/safavid-iran">Social Hierarchy of Safavid Iran</a></li></ul></section>
